hey everyone it's sarah thread sterner  sorry and calm and in this video we're  going to go over how to solve dopamine  calculations and after you watch this  YouTube video you can access the free  quiz that will give you more practice  over these calculations so let's get  started first let's do a quick  pharmacology review over dopamine this  drug is a beta adrenergic agonist which  means it acts as a vasopressor with  positive inotropic effects now what does  this mean well these oppressors what  they do is they perform vasoconstriction  they really press down that vessel make  it narrow and whenever that happens that  will help increase blood pressure  increase the semuc vascular resistance  and increase our cardiac output so  dopamine can be used in some patients in  certain types of shock where they're  experiencing severe hypotension their  cardiac output is low or their systemic  vascular resistance is low and you throw  on the dopamine and that can help  increase those values in addition  because of dopamine's positive inotropic  effects it increases the heart's  contractility which means it helps the  heart pump stronger  another thing dopamine does in low doses  about 0.5 to 3 mics per kilogram per  minute is that it can actually cause  renal vasodilation so the vessels that  feed our kidneys will dilate which will  increase perfusion to the kidneys this  can help increase our urinary output the  reason it does this is the way that the  dopamine affects those dopaminergic  receptors in the kidneys now whenever we  start getting into high doses of  dopamine about 10 mics per kilogram per  minute it actually has the opposite  effect you'll have constriction of those  vessels that feed the kidney so only in  low doses do you have the dilation now  dopamine is administered IV and it's  best given through a central line now  why is that why don't we want to give it  through a central line rather than  the basic IV cannula round well dopamine  is a major vasoconstrictor so if  extravasation occurs where dopamine  accidentally leaks into that surrounding  tissue it can lead to tissue ischemia  necrosis so as a nurse you've really got  to watch that infusion site and if this  does occur what medication can be given  to help in a sense reverse those  vasoconstrictive effects you can give  santal Amin which causes vasodilation so  remember that drug for your vasopressors  dopamine the infusion rate is anywhere  between 2 to 20 Mike's per kilogram per  minute this infusion rate really depends  on the parameters set by the physician  as a nurse you will be titrating this  drug based on whatever the physician  wants that patient in and some other  things you want to remember with this  drug the patient will need to be on  continuous EKG monitoring looking at  that rhythm dopamine can significantly  increase the heart rate so looking at  the heart rate looking at their blood  pressure monitoring that constantly  their urinary output as well because the  way that dopamine can influence the  perfusion to the kidneys and of course  watching that IV site now let's do some  practice dopamine calculations our  problem says a patient is ordered to  start an IV dopamine drip at 10 Mike's  per kilogram per minute the patient  weighs 55 kilograms you have a bag of  dopamine that reads 800 milligrams per  500 MLS what will you set the IV pump  rate at so to solve this problem we need  to know three things first the patient's  weight because dopamine is weight base  and we're told the patient weighs 55  kilograms second the dose how much is  ordered and what's ordered is 10  micrograms per kilogram per minute and  then third what is available to us this  bag of dopamine that we have how much  does it contain well we're told we have  a 500 milliliter bag that has 800  milligrams of dopamine now what we're  trying to get to is the milliliters per  hour because this is what we're gonna  set our IV pump out so it can achieve  this order dose based on this patient's  weight and what the physician wants so  to solve these problems I like to use  dimensional analysis and if you're not  familiar with how to set up dimensional  analysis I have a whole series you can  access that will show you how to do that  and I will be rounding these answers at  the end to the nearest tenth but for  your nursing school always follow their  rounding rules so what I like to do is I  like to start out with the way and this  one's easy because normally you have to  go to pounds and kilograms but we're  already in kilograms so we'll start out  right with that so 55 kilograms is how  much our patient weighs now what's  ordered is it says for every kilogram  this patient weighs they need 10  micrograms per minute of dopamine more  patient weighs 55 kilograms so one  kilogram is equal to ten micrograms per  minute because that's what they want  so we're kilograms cancels out and we're  trying to get to milliliters per hour  okay so right now we're in micrograms  because we're talking about 10  micrograms and our bag is in milligrams  so we're gonna have to convert because  we need to get there so we know that a  thousand micrograms equals 1 milligram  thousand micrograms equals 1 milligram  that cancels out our micrograms so now  we're in milligrams now we can plug in  our dose that's available so we have a  bag that has 800 milligrams of dopamine  and 500 MLS well we're trying to get to  milliliters per hour but we're at  milliliters per minute right now that's  what's left over so we're gonna go ahead  and solve so we're gonna multiply  everything at the top and everything at  the bottom so 55 times 10 times 1 times  500 is 200 and  seventy-five thousand and then one times  one times one thousand times 800 is 800  thousand then we're going to divide that  out when we divide that we get zero  point three four three seven five  milliliters per minute but we're going  to get two milliliters per hour so how  we're gonna do that well we're gonna set  this up over here we know that there are  60 minutes in one hour and with this for  every minute we're gonna give point  three four three seven five meals so one  minute equal to 0.34 375 milliliters  minutes cancels out we're now in  milliliters per hour so 60 times point  three four 375 gives us 20 point six two  five and when you multiply that at the  bottom of course that gives you one and  you divide that out we're gonna round to  the nearest tenth will give us 20 point  six milliliters per hour that's our  answer our next problem says a patient  is ordered to start an IV dopamine drip  at five micrograms per kilogram per  minute the patient weighs a hundred and  eighty pounds  you have a bag of dopamine that reads  400 milligrams per 250 MLS what will you  set the IV pump at so to solve this  problem we need to know those three  basic things that we discussed before we  need to know the weight which is 180  pounds the dose which is five micrograms  per kilogram per minute and what's  available to us we have a 250 ml bag of  dopamine that contains 400 milligrams  and we're trying to get two milliliters  per hour because that's what we're gonna  set our pump at so let's start out with  our weight and here we're gonna have to  convert to pound from pounds to  kilograms so the patient weighs a  hundred and  and we know according to the metric  table that there's 2.2 pounds in one  kilogram so 2.2 pounds in one kilogram  so our pounds cancels out we're  following kilograms so now let's plug in  our dose the dose says for every  kilogram my patient waves they need 5  micrograms per minute of dopamine so one  kilogram is going to be equal to 5  micrograms per minute kilograms cancels  out here we're trying to get milliliters  per hour now we need to do some  converting because our bag is in  milligrams and right now we're still in  micrograms so we know from the metric  table that there's a thousand micrograms  in one milligram so micrograms cancels  out and now we can plug in what we have  available to us and we have a bag it  contains 400 milligrams and 250 MLS so  we're gonna go ahead and solve we're  gonna multiply everything at the top so  180 times 1 times 5 times 1 times 250 is  225 thousand and then multiply  everything at the bottom 1 times 2 point  2 times 1 times 1000 times 400 is  880,000 and then when we divide that out  we will get 0.25 5 6 8 1 8 repeating so  that is milliliters per minute but we  need to get to milliliters per hour so  we're gonna do a little bit more  dimensional analysis and we know that  there are 60 minutes in one hour and we  know with this what we've calculated for  every minute they will be receiving 0.25  0.25 5/6 a18 repeating so one minute is  equal to that  and when we multiply that at the top at  the bottom what are we going to get  we're going to get fifteen point three  four zero nine PT and then we're gonna  round to the nearest tenth and our  answer will be fifteen point three  milliliters per hour okay so that wraps  up how to solve IV dopamine drip  calculations thank you so much for  watching don't forget to take the free  quiz and to subscribe to our channel for  more videos

